<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Majorca</h1><p class="st-meta">1076 CE – 1115 CE (approx.) · capital Madina Mayurqa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Balearic Islands as an independent taifa, a base for corsairs whose raids provoked the Pisan-Catalan crusade of 1113-1115.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearics</a> had been ruled from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-denia">Taifa of Dénia</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-muqtadir">al-Muqtadir</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-zaragoza">Taifa of Zaragoza</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> in 1076, the island governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-al-murtada">Abd Allah al-Murtada</a> declared himself independent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madina-mayurqa">Madina Mayurqa</a>, the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The islands lived from farming, fishing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trade, and from corsair raids on Christian shipping and the coasts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-provence">Provence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, which brought in captives and booty. Al-Murtada was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mubashshir-nasir-al-dawla">Mubashshir Nasir al-Dawla</a>, a freedman who ruled until 1114.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1113-1115 a fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisans</a> and Catalans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iii-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona</a> sacked <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ebusus">Ibiza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madina-mayurqa">Madina Mayurqa</a>, but withdrew. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> then occupied the islands, which later formed the base of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banu-ghaniya-majorca">Banu Ghaniya Majorca</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-majorca.jpg" alt="Taifa of Majorca" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_Mallorca.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1076 CE – 1115 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madina Mayurqa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abd Allah al-Murtada, Mubashshir Nasir al-Dawla</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Spain</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Taifa of Mallorca, Taifa of the Balearics</dd></dl></aside></div>
